**The Test Pyramid.** A healthy suite is mostly fast tests at the bottom and few slow tests at the top:

```
        /\        E2E / UI        ← few, slow, brittle (full system, real browser)
       /  \       Integration     ← some, medium (real DB via Testcontainers, full context)
      /    \      Slice tests      ← more (@WebMvcTest, @DataJpaTest — one layer only)
     /______\     Unit tests       ← MANY, milliseconds (pure logic + Mockito, no Spring)
```

**Unit tests — Mockito, no Spring context.** The fastest tests. No Spring, no DB. You instantiate the class under test and replace its collaborators with mocks. `@Mock` creates a stub returning defaults; `@InjectMocks` builds the subject and injects the mocks; `when(...).thenReturn(...)` programs behaviour; `verify(...)` asserts an interaction happened.

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class FeeCalculatorTest {

    @Mock  FeeRateRepository rateRepo;          // collaborator replaced by a stub
    @InjectMocks FeeCalculator calculator;       // subject under test, mocks injected

    @Test
    void calculatesBrokerageAtPointZeroThreePercent() {
        when(rateRepo.rateFor("NSE")).thenReturn(new BigDecimal("0.0003"));

        BigDecimal fee = calculator.brokerage("NSE", new BigDecimal("100000"));

        assertThat(fee).isEqualByComparingTo("30.00");   // 100000 * 0.0003
        verify(rateRepo).rateFor("NSE");                 // interaction verified
    }
}
```

**`@WebMvcTest` — the web slice only.** Loads only the controller layer + Spring MVC infrastructure (`MockMvc`, Jackson, validators, `@ControllerAdvice`). It does NOT load services, repositories, or the DB — you supply them as `@MockBean`. Use it to verify JSON (de)serialization, request validation, status codes, and error responses.

```java
@WebMvcTest(TradeController.class)
class TradeControllerTest {

    @Autowired  MockMvc mvc;
    @MockBean   TradeService tradeService;     // service is mocked — only the web layer is real

    @Test
    void returns400WhenQuantityNegative() throws Exception {
        mvc.perform(post("/api/v1/trades")
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("{\"symbol\":\"INFY\",\"quantity\":-5}"))
           .andExpect(status().isBadRequest())
           .andExpect(jsonPath("$.errors[0].field").value("quantity"));
    }
}
```

**`@DataJpaTest` — the JPA slice only.** Loads only JPA: entities, repositories, `EntityManager`, and a transaction that rolls back after each test. By default it swaps in an embedded H2 DB; point it at a real engine with `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = NONE)` + Testcontainers. Use it to test complex JPQL / derived queries / native SQL.

```java
@DataJpaTest
class TradeRepositoryTest {

    @Autowired TradeRepository repo;

    @Test
    void findsUnsettledTradesBeforeCutoff() {
        repo.save(new Trade("T1", Status.PENDING, LocalDate.now().minusDays(2)));
        repo.save(new Trade("T2", Status.SETTLED, LocalDate.now().minusDays(2)));

        List<Trade> pending = repo.findUnsettledBefore(LocalDate.now());  // custom JPQL

        assertThat(pending).extracting(Trade::getId).containsExactly("T1");
    }
}
```

**`@SpringBootTest` — the full context.** Boots the entire application context (all beans, config, web server if `webEnvironment=RANDOM_PORT`). Slow, so reserve it for integration tests that must exercise multiple layers together.

**Testcontainers — real infrastructure in Docker.** Instead of mocking the DB or using H2 (which lies about PostgreSQL behaviour), Testcontainers spins up a real PostgreSQL / Redis / Kafka container for the test and tears it down after. This is the only way to catch dialect-specific SQL, migration, and index bugs.

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Testcontainers
class SettlementIntegrationTest {

    @Container
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres =
        new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:16-alpine");

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void props(DynamicPropertyRegistry r) {
        r.add("spring.datasource.url", postgres::getJdbcUrl);
        r.add("spring.datasource.username", postgres::getUsername);
        r.add("spring.datasource.password", postgres::getPassword);
    }
    // Flyway runs real migrations against real PostgreSQL before the test body
}
```

**TDD — Red → Green → Refactor.** Write a failing test first (Red), write the minimum code to pass (Green), then clean up the design while tests stay green (Refactor). This guarantees every line of production code is covered and designed to be testable.

